      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>NPS and UPS Pension Deductions on Retrospective Arrears</h2>
        <p>
          For central government employees recruited after January 1, 2004—governed either by the <strong>National Pension System (NPS)</strong> or the newly operationalized <strong>Unified Pension Scheme (UPS)</strong>—arrear payouts are subject to statutory social security deductions at source:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>Employee Contribution Adjustment (10%):</strong> Because the statutory pension contribution equals 10% of (Basic Pay &plus; DA), and the employee previously contributed 10% only on the 7th CPC baseline, exactly <strong>10% of the net basic pay arrears</strong> is deducted at source and credited directly to the individual's Permanent Retirement Account Number (PRAN).</li>
          <li><strong>Matching Government Contribution (14% or 18.5%):</strong> The Central Government simultaneously deposits its matching employer share (14% under NPS or 18.5% under UPS) into the employee's corpus, substantially boosting cumulative retirement assets without impacting monthly take-home cash.</li>
          <li><strong>Old Pension Scheme (OPS) Exemption:</strong> Employees governed under the pre-2004 Old Pension Scheme (including armed forces personnel and GPF subscribers) do not undergo mandatory pension deductions from arrears, receiving the full net basic differential directly into their bank accounts.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

